\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 + (id <NSDecimalNumberBehaviors>)\b defaultBehavior\b0  \
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Returns the way that arithmetic methods, like \b decimalNumberByAdding:\b0 , round off and handle error conditions. \
\fs14\
\fs28 By default, the arithmetic methods do not round numbers off. They assume that your need for precision does not exceed 38 significant digits. And they raise exceptions when they try to divide by zero, or when they produce a number that is too big or small to be represented.\
\fs14\
\fs28 If this default behavior doesn\'27t suit your application, you should use methods that let you specify the behavior, like \b decimalNumberByAdding:withBehavior.\b0  If you find yourself using a particular behavior consistently, you can specify a different default behavior with \b setDefaultBehavior:\b0 .\
\fs14\
\fs46\
